.TH CTPOOL_DESTROY 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ctpool_destroy \- destroy a thread pool and invalidate the handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ctpool_destroy(pool);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Drains or cancels every task that is still pending. It then joins every
worker thread. It waits for every resolved use of the handle that is
still in flight to stop, and frees every resource of the pool. The macro
then sets the pool handle to CTPOOL_INVALID.

The macro runs a drain shutdown by itself when the caller called neither
ctpool_shutdown_drain() nor ctpool_shutdown_immediate() beforehand.
Every task in the queue therefore finishes before the library frees the
pool.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool  Thread pool variable to destroy (set to CTPOOL_INVALID after
      destruction).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
It is safe to call when pool is CTPOOL_INVALID. The macro then does
nothing.

A call on a stale handle is a FATAL ERROR, which is abort() and
SIGABRT. A stale handle is one that an earlier call already destroyed,
or that another thread destroys at the same time. This is never a
silent double free. The handle carries a generation tag, and the
library checks that tag before it touches the pool. It therefore always
detects such a value, and never dereferences it.

A call on pool from inside a task is a FATAL ERROR as well. That holds
when the task runs on one of the worker threads of pool. A call from the
on_complete callback of that task is the same. The reason is related.
That worker is still on its way back through its own dispatch loop. It
would touch the mutex, the condition variables and the struct of the
pool again, immediately after this call freed them. Leave the destroy
to another thread instead, or run it after the task returns.

The macro evaluates pool exactly once. It must be a modifiable lvalue,
such as a variable or an element of an array, so an expression with a
side effect, such as handles[i++], destroys and clears one element.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_construct (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_drain (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_immediate (3)
